Changes made for erpc worker:
- The trading key arrives on a pipe named by ERPC_WORKER_KEY_FD and is held
  as one shared keypair; the wallet.json file, the vanity key generator and
  the per-trade copies of the secret key bytes are gone.
- The process marks itself undumpable on Linux before the key is read.
- Settings come from the environment only; .env and .env.erpc are no longer
  read.
- The API listens on 127.0.0.1 unless API_BIND says otherwise, every request
  needs API_TOKEN (compared in constant time), the permissive CORS layer is
  gone, and /healthz answers without a token.
- There is no public RPC fallback: SOLANA_RPC_ENDPOINT or ERPC_API_KEY is
  required. URLs are logged without their query or credentials, and a failed
  webhook request is logged without the webhook URL.
- Explorer links in notifications point at EXPLORER_URL when it is set, and
  are plain signatures and addresses otherwise.
- Trading no longer pays fees for nothing: the take-profit target includes
  the transaction fees and is met by what the exact-out sell is sure to
  bring in; transactions ask for preflight simulation; a sell that may still
  land is settled before another is signed, and the wallet's balance is read
  before every sell, a lower read never letting a position be sold below its
  target; a failed sell is retried after a growing wait by a monitor that
  keeps watching while a sell runs, and given up after five sells that
  landed and failed or twenty failures of any kind, which pauses buying; a
  position is claimed by one seller at a time; a pool is bought once, a
  token still held or being bought is not bought again, and buys under way
  count against max_positions; tokens with a freeze authority or a
  Token-2022 extension that can stop transfers are not bought; a sell sure
  to bring in no more than its fee is replaced by a burn; a close is retried
  until the token account is gone, up to the give-up limits, burns exactly
  what it was decided for, and takes a missing account for closed only after
  a close of its own went out; a buy is settled before its slot is given
  back, and tracked when it lands late; only WSOL/token pools are bought or
  restored, and restore takes the deepest one and skips tokens that cannot
  be sold; sell_only mode buys nothing; restored positions are sold only
  once trading starts; PUT /api/config refuses settings that would trade
  badly. The default minimum pool liquidity is 1 SOL.
- Graduations are traded as well as pools made by hand: a create_pool that
  pump.fun's program calls is seen among the inner instructions, accounts
  loaded from address lookup tables are resolved, pools with the token as
  the base and WSOL as the quote are bought, sold and restored with the
  token's own program on its side, and a graduation is bought from its
  create_pool instruction, without reads first or a simulation. The pool
  account is read with coin_creator before is_mayhem_mode, as on mainnet,
  and a pool's creator is read from create_pool's third account (the
  second is PumpSwap's global config); a graduation's creator is its coin
  creator. graduated_only leaves pools made by hand out.
- With SHREDS_ENDPOINT, pump.fun migrations are read from the Shreds stream
  before they run, their lookup tables resolved, and timed against Geyser at
  processed and their slot confirmed (a slot subscription in config.jsonc);
  GET /api/timing reports those times and how many slots after its pool each
  buy landed. buy_on_shreds buys a graduation from the Shreds stream; a pool
  seen twice is counted once against its creator. A buy can pay a priority
  fee (priority_fee_micro_lamports, compute_unit_limit), counted in its cost.
- POST /api/bans/trace bans the wallet that first funded each banned
  creator and the other wallets it funded lately, leaving out wallets with
  long histories, funders paying many wallets, and the bot's own wallet;
  traced creators are kept in Redis.
- The latency monitor (getBlockTime per slot) and the map of transactions it
  read are gone: it was switched off for exhausting the RPC, and GET
  /api/timing measures from the streams instead. Dependencies nothing used
  are gone with it.
- The package and binary are named after the app; reqwest uses rustls
  instead of the system's OpenSSL, so the app cross-builds for Linux and
  needs no libssl on the server.
